15 0 obj
<< /A 46 0 R /Alt (Flow diagram showing 26 participants with non-random allocation to an intervention group with 14 participants, one withdrawal, 13 completing, and a control group with 12 participants, all completing post-assessment.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 32 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
16 0 obj
<< /A 47 0 R /Alt (Stacked bar chart compares physical activity intensity distribution between a five-hour camp and a two-hour physical education session for 13 participants, showing values in both minutes and percent. Intensity categories include sedentary, light, moderate, and vigorous, with sedentary and light activity higher during camp, and moderate activity higher during PE.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 37 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 48 0 R /Alt (Line chart illustrating daily Fitbit activity minutes from June 9th to July 23rd, with weekends highlighted. Weekday average is 166.2 minutes, weekend average is 135.6 minutes, and p-value is zero point zero three nine.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 38 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 49 0 R /Alt (Line graph illustrating app engagement over eight weeks, with number of entries on the y-axis and weeks on the x-axis. Entries drop from two hundred seventy-one in week one to four in week eight.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 50 0 R /Alt (Bar chart and boxplot compare feedback types. Positive reinforcement forms the majority at sixty-seven percent with five hundred seventy-one instances. Boxplot shows positive reinforcement has the highest average count per student at thirty-five point seven, with standard deviation twenty-three.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 51 0 R /Alt (Line graph with three panels displaying changes over time for weight, BMI, and waist circumference in control \(orange\) and ProudMe \(purple\) groups. The control group shows gradual increases in all measures, while ProudMe\220s weight and BMI remain relatively stable, and waist circumference decreases then rises post-intervention. Error bars are present, and p-values are provided for statistical comparisons at each time point.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 52 0 R /Alt (Four grouped bar charts display accelerometer-measured physical activity for the Control \(yellow\) and ProudMe \(purple\) groups at pre- and post-intervention, including Light PA, Moderate PA, Vigorous PA, and Total MVPA in minutes with standard errors. Significant time effects were observed for Moderate PA, Vigorous PA, and Total MVPA. Significant group effects were observed for Light PA, Moderate PA, and Total MVPA, with the Control group showing higher overall activity levels than the ProudMe group. Group × Time interactions were not significant for any physical activity outcomes.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 40 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 53 0 R /Alt (Four-panel line graph compares Control and ProudMe groups on self-reported health behaviors before and after an intervention. Q1 shows days with sixty or more minutes of activity slightly decrease for Control and rise for ProudMe. Q2 depicts stable leisure-time MVPA for Control but a decrease for ProudMe. Fruit and vegetable intake remains stable in Control and declines in ProudMe. Screen time score declines for Control and rises for ProudMe. P-values indicate no significant group-by-time interactions.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 41 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 54 0 R /Alt (Two line graphs compare self-efficacy and PAF knowledge before and after intervention for Control and ProudMe groups. Self-efficacy slightly decreases for Control and remains stable for ProudMe. PAF knowledge modestly increases for both groups, meeting at the same post-intervention mean. P-values indicate no statistically significant differences for either outcome.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 42 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
